"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[26338],{226338:n=>{n.exports="# PL/pgSQL — Procedures, UDFs, Scripting\n\nRedshift PL/pgSQL → DBSQL SQL scripting. Covers stored procedures, UDFs, variables, control flow (`IF`/`LOOP`/`FOR`/`WHILE`), cursors, dynamic SQL, and error handling.\n\n> For DBSQL stored procedure syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/stored-procedures.md`.\n\n## REDSHIFT\n\n### Dollar quoting — strip `$$`\n\nRedshift (PostgreSQL) wraps function/procedure bodies in dollar quotes (`$$ ... $$` or `$tag$ ... $tag$`). DBSQL uses inline expression bodies for SQL functions and `BEGIN ... END` blocks for procedures. **Always strip** `$$` and the `LANGUAGE` clause when translating.\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift function\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION f_double(n INT)\nRETURNS INT STABLE\nAS $$ SELECT $1 * 2 $$ LANGUAGE sql;\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.f_double(n INT)\nRETURNS INT\nRETURN n * 2;\n```\n\nPositional `$1`, `$2` parameter references in Redshift SQL UDFs become named parameter references in DBSQL — use the parameter names from the function signature.\n\n### Stored Procedures (PL/pgSQL)\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE sp_my_proc(\n    IN  p_param1 INT,\n    IN  p_param2 VARCHAR DEFAULT 'default',\n    OUT p_output INT\n)\nLANGUAGE plpgsql\nAS $$\nBEGIN\n    p_output := p_param1 * 2;\nEND;\n$$;\n\n-- DBSQL (Unity Catalog three-part name; LANGUAGE SQL replaces plpgsql)\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_catalog.my_schema.sp_my_proc(\n    IN  p_param1 INT,\n    IN  p_param2 STRING DEFAULT 'default',\n    OUT p_output INT\n)\nLANGUAGE SQL\nSQL SECURITY INVOKER\nAS BEGIN\n    SET p_output = p_param1 * 2;\nEND;\n```\n\n**Security context (`SQL SECURITY INVOKER`)**: Always include `SQL SECURITY INVOKER` — it is required and the only accepted value in DBSQL. Omitting it causes `MISSING_CLAUSES_FOR_OPERATION: SQL SECURITY` (SQLSTATE 42601). Redshift procedures default to `SECURITY INVOKER`, so the semantics match. If the Redshift source used `SECURITY DEFINER`, DBSQL has no equivalent — flag it in a migration comment.\n\n**PL/pgSQL assignment (`:=`) → DBSQL `SET`**: Redshift uses `var := expr;`. DBSQL uses `SET var = expr;` inside compound statements (no `VAR` keyword — `SET VAR` is the session-variable form and is not valid inside procedure bodies).\n\n**`BEGIN ATOMIC` vs bare `BEGIN`**: Both are valid procedure-body openers in DBSQL. `BEGIN ATOMIC` wraps the body in a single transaction that rolls back on failure — prefer it for procedures that mutate multiple tables and must stay all-or-nothing. Bare `BEGIN ... END` is a plain compound statement: each DML statement commits independently, so a mid-body failure leaves earlier writes in place. Match the source's intent — Redshift procedures run inside an implicit transaction, so `BEGIN ATOMIC` is the closer behavioral match in most cases.\n\n```sql\n-- DML-only procedure (no RETURNS TABLE)\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_catalog.my_schema.proc_name(IN p1 INT)\nLANGUAGE SQL\nSQL SECURITY INVOKER\nBEGIN ATOMIC\n    INSERT INTO t SELECT p1;\n    UPDATE t SET status = 'done' WHERE id = p1;\nEND;\n```\n\n### `RAISE NOTICE` / `RAISE EXCEPTION` → SIGNAL\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nRAISE NOTICE 'processed % rows', v_count;\nRAISE EXCEPTION 'invalid input: %', p_value;\n\n-- DBSQL\n-- RAISE NOTICE has no client-side notice channel in DBSQL. Two workarounds:\n--   (a) Drop the statement if the notice was purely informational.\n--   (b) Append to an audit log table to preserve the message for post-run inspection:\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.procedure_log (proc_name, ts, message)\nVALUES ('sp_my_proc', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CONCAT('processed ', v_count, ' rows'));\n\n-- RAISE EXCEPTION → SIGNAL\nSIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = CONCAT('invalid input: ', p_value);\n```\n\nSQLSTATE `'45000'` is the generic user-defined exception state. Use the audit-log pattern when the source relied on `RAISE NOTICE` for monitoring or debugging a long-running procedure; otherwise drop.\n\n### User-Defined Functions (UDFs)\n\n**SQL UDFs** — translate directly after stripping `$$ ... $$ LANGUAGE sql`:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift SQL UDF\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION f_full_name(first VARCHAR, last VARCHAR)\nRETURNS VARCHAR\nSTABLE\nAS $$ SELECT $1 || ' ' || $2 $$ LANGUAGE sql;\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.f_full_name(first STRING, last STRING)\nRETURNS STRING\nRETURN first || ' ' || last;\n```\n\nRedshift volatility hints (`IMMUTABLE`, `STABLE`, `VOLATILE`) have no DBSQL equivalent — drop them.\n\n**Python UDFs (`LANGUAGE plpythonu`)** — can be migrated to a Unity Catalog Python UDF when the body is expressible with standard-library Python. The function signature, body, and call sites translate directly; only the `LANGUAGE plpythonu` declaration needs to be replaced with `LANGUAGE PYTHON` and the body inlined in `$$...$$` quotes that DBSQL does accept for Python UDFs:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION f_parse_json(s VARCHAR)\nRETURNS VARCHAR STABLE\nAS $$\n    import json\n    return json.loads(s).get('id')\n$$ LANGUAGE plpythonu;\n\n-- DBSQL (Unity Catalog Python UDF — body preserved verbatim)\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.f_parse_json(s STRING)\nRETURNS STRING\nLANGUAGE PYTHON\nAS $$\nimport json\nreturn json.loads(s).get('id')\n$$;\n```\n\n**Before translating**, check the body for Redshift-specific Python constraints the source may have worked around (sandboxed import set, scalar-only return, no network I/O). DBSQL Python UDFs have a different sandbox — body assumptions may need review. If the body imports non-standard packages (NumPy, Pandas) or is not scalar-in / scalar-out, port to a **PySpark UDF** in a notebook cell instead and leave a TODO:\n\n```sql\n-- TODO: manual review required — plpythonu UDF uses <package> / table-return; port to a PySpark UDF in a notebook\n```\n\n**Lambda UDFs (`CREATE EXTERNAL FUNCTION ... LAMBDA`)** — no in-SQL equivalent. The closest migration path is a Unity Catalog **Python UDF** that invokes the same Lambda via `boto3.client('lambda').invoke(...)` and unwraps the payload. Include the Lambda ARN and invocation parameters from the source. If the user does not want a cross-service call from a UDF, flag as TODO and surface the original Lambda business logic so it can be re-implemented in SQL or PySpark.\n\n**UDF call sites — qualify with three-part name.** When migrating a query or procedure that calls a user-defined function, rewrite the call with the migrated UDF's three-part name: `f_holiday(d)` → `my_catalog.my_schema.f_holiday(d)`. Bare unqualified UDF references will fail to resolve in DBSQL once the function is moved to Unity Catalog.\n\n### Cursors (literal translation)\n\nRedshift cursors appear inside PL/pgSQL procedures as `FOR row IN SELECT ... LOOP ... END LOOP` or via `DECLARE cursor_name CURSOR FOR ... OPEN cursor_name`. Prefer a set-based rewrite (see `03-query-and-dml.md`). Use literal translation only when row-by-row semantics are strictly required.\n\nCursor syntax is supported in DBSQL warehouses (DBR 18.1+) and in SQL scripting:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift PL/pgSQL cursor loop\nFOR rec IN SELECT id FROM source_table LOOP\n    UPDATE other_table SET processed = 1 WHERE id = rec.id;\nEND LOOP;\n\n-- DBSQL cursor translation\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE v_id INT;\n    DECLARE done INT DEFAULT 0;\n    DECLARE cur CURSOR FOR SELECT id FROM source_table;\n    DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND SET done = 1;\n\n    OPEN cur;\n    fetch_loop: LOOP\n        FETCH cur INTO v_id;\n        IF done = 1 THEN LEAVE fetch_loop; END IF;\n        UPDATE other_table SET processed = 1 WHERE id = v_id;\n    END LOOP fetch_loop;\n    CLOSE cur;\nEND;\n```\n\n### Error Handling (EXCEPTION WHEN ... THEN → DECLARE HANDLER)\n\nRedshift PL/pgSQL uses `EXCEPTION WHEN ... THEN`. DBSQL uses `DECLARE ... HANDLER FOR ...` declared at the top of a `BEGIN ... END` block.\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nBEGIN\n    INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, amount) VALUES (1, 100.00);\nEXCEPTION WHEN OTHERS THEN\n    RAISE NOTICE 'insert failed: %', SQLERRM;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\n    BEGIN\n        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'insert failed';\n    END;\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.orders (customer_id, amount) VALUES (1, 100.00);\nEND;\n```\n\n**Redshift only supports `WHEN OTHERS`**: unlike standard PostgreSQL, Redshift PL/pgSQL does **not** support named condition handlers (`WHEN unique_violation`, `WHEN foreign_key_violation`, etc.) — the only valid condition in a Redshift `EXCEPTION` block is `WHEN OTHERS`. If the source wants to branch on error type, it has to read `SQLSTATE` inside the handler body. Translate to DBSQL `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION` (which catches all exceptions, matching Redshift `WHEN OTHERS`) and, if needed, branch inside the handler via `GET DIAGNOSTICS`.\n\n**Key differences:**\n\n| Redshift                         | DBSQL                                                 | Notes                                                                                              |\n| -------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `EXCEPTION WHEN OTHERS THEN ...` | `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION BEGIN ... END` | Handler at top of block; `EXIT` means exit the block on error. Redshift only allows `WHEN OTHERS`. |\n| `RAISE EXCEPTION 'msg'`          | `SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'msg'`    |                                                                                                    |\n| `RAISE` (re-raise current)       | `RESIGNAL`                                            | Re-raises the current exception inside a handler                                                   |\n| `SQLERRM`                        | `MESSAGE_TEXT` (via `GET DIAGNOSTICS`)                | `GET DIAGNOSTICS CONDITION 1 msg = MESSAGE_TEXT`                                                   |\n| `SQLSTATE`                       | `RETURNED_SQLSTATE` (via `GET DIAGNOSTICS`)           | 5-char SQLSTATE string                                                                             |\n\n**CONTINUE vs EXIT handlers:**\n\n- `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER` — exits the block on error (equivalent to the common Redshift `EXCEPTION WHEN ... THEN` pattern that aborts the procedure).\n- `DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER` — resumes after the failing statement. Used primarily in cursor loops (`HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND`).\n\n### Variables\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift PL/pgSQL\nDECLARE\n    v_count INT := 0;\n    v_name  VARCHAR(100);\nBEGIN\n    v_name := 'value';\n    SELECT MAX(id) INTO v_count FROM t;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE v_count INT DEFAULT 0;\n    DECLARE v_name STRING;\n    SET v_name = 'value';\n    SET v_count = (SELECT MAX(id) FROM t);\nEND;\n```\n\n**Key differences:**\n\n- Redshift `DECLARE` block appears before `BEGIN`; DBSQL `DECLARE` statements go **inside** `BEGIN ... END`, immediately after `BEGIN`.\n- Assignment: Redshift `:=`, DBSQL `SET ... = ...`.\n- `SELECT ... INTO var FROM t` (Redshift PL/pgSQL): DBSQL uses `SET var = (SELECT ... FROM t);`. Multi-column form: `SET (a, b) = (SELECT col1, col2 FROM t WHERE id = 1);`.\n\n**Variable assignment edge cases:**\n\n- If the `SELECT` returns **0 rows**, DBSQL's scalar subquery evaluates to `NULL` and the variable is overwritten — unlike PL/pgSQL `SELECT INTO`, which leaves the variable unchanged. If the source relied on the PL/pgSQL behavior (e.g. preserving a declared default), wrap with `COALESCE`: `SET v_count = COALESCE((SELECT MAX(id) FROM t), v_count);`.\n- If the `SELECT` returns **multiple rows**, DBSQL raises an error. PL/pgSQL `SELECT INTO` silently takes the first row — if the source relies on that, add an explicit `ORDER BY ... LIMIT 1` or use `MIN`/`MAX`.\n\n### Parameter modes\n\n`IN`, `OUT`, `INOUT` — identical syntax in both dialects. Calling a procedure with OUT parameter:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nCALL sp_my_proc(10, 'default', :result);\n\n-- DBSQL (session-level call from a notebook cell)\nDECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE result INT;\nCALL my_catalog.my_schema.sp_my_proc(10, 'default', result);\n```\n\n### Multi-result-set mapping\n\nDBSQL procedures surface only the **last** SELECT statement's result to the caller. Redshift PL/pgSQL procedures return result sets via `INOUT refcursor` parameters (or by passing a temp-table name as `INOUT varchar`) — neither has a direct DBSQL equivalent.\n\nConsolidate before writing the cell:\n\n- **Option A (preferred when schemas are compatible)**: Combine with `UNION ALL` and a discriminator column. Flag the discriminator in a comment — it was not in the original schema.\n- **Option B**: Restructure to a single final SELECT, staging intermediate results in temp tables or CTEs.\n- **Option C (last resort)**: Select the authoritative result set and document what was discarded.\n\n### Control flow\n\n| Redshift                                        | DBSQL                                                             | Notes                                                                                                                                                         |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `IF cond THEN ... END IF;`                      | `IF cond THEN ... END IF;`                                        | Identical                                                                                                                                                     |\n| `IF cond THEN ... ELSIF cond2 THEN ... END IF;` | `IF cond THEN ... ELSEIF cond2 THEN ... END IF;`                  | `ELSIF` (Redshift) → `ELSEIF` (DBSQL) — one letter difference                                                                                                 |\n| `IF cond THEN ... ELSE ... END IF;`             | `IF cond THEN ... ELSE ... END IF;`                               | Identical                                                                                                                                                     |\n| `CASE WHEN ... THEN ... END CASE;` (statement)  | `CASE WHEN ... THEN ... END CASE;`                                | Statement-level CASE supported in DBSQL scripting                                                                                                             |\n| `LOOP ... END LOOP;`                            | `LOOP ... END LOOP;`                                              | Infinite loop; use `EXIT` / `LEAVE` to terminate                                                                                                              |\n| `WHILE cond LOOP ... END LOOP;`                 | `WHILE cond DO ... END WHILE;`                                    | Keyword change: `LOOP ... END LOOP` → `DO ... END WHILE`                                                                                                      |\n| `FOR i IN 1..10 LOOP ... END LOOP;`             | `FOR i AS SELECT num FROM range(1, 11) AS t(num) DO ... END FOR;` | DBSQL `FOR` iterates over a query result; use `range(start, end_exclusive)` to simulate integer ranges                                                        |\n| `FOR rec IN SELECT ... LOOP ... END LOOP;`      | `FOR rec AS SELECT ... DO ... END FOR;`                           | DBSQL `FOR` supports query iteration directly; keyword is `AS` (not `IN`), and block ends with `END FOR`                                                      |\n| `EXIT [label] [WHEN cond]`                      | `LEAVE label;` (conditional via IF)                               | `LEAVE` requires a label — if the Redshift source is unlabeled, add a label to the enclosing loop during translation. `WHEN` clause must be lifted into an IF |\n| `CONTINUE [label] [WHEN cond]`                  | `ITERATE label;`                                                  | `ITERATE` requires a label — same rule as `EXIT` → `LEAVE`                                                                                                    |\n| `RETURN;` (PL/pgSQL procedure)                  | `RETURN;`                                                         | Same                                                                                                                                                          |\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift IF / ELSIF / ELSE\nIF v_count > 0 THEN\n    UPDATE t SET status = 'active';\nELSIF v_count = 0 THEN\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (1, 'inactive');\nELSE\n    RAISE NOTICE 'unexpected count: %', v_count;\nEND IF;\n\n-- DBSQL — note ELSIF → ELSEIF\nIF v_count > 0 THEN\n    UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.t SET status = 'active';\nELSEIF v_count = 0 THEN\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (1, 'inactive');\nELSE\n    SELECT CONCAT('unexpected count: ', CAST(v_count AS STRING));\nEND IF;\n```\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift WHILE\nDECLARE\n    i INT := 0;\nBEGIN\n    WHILE i < 10 LOOP\n        INSERT INTO t VALUES (i);\n        i := i + 1;\n    END LOOP;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL — LOOP→DO, END LOOP→END WHILE, := → SET\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE i INT DEFAULT 0;\n    WHILE i < 10 DO\n        INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (i);\n        SET i = i + 1;\n    END WHILE;\nEND;\n```\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift FOR integer\nFOR i IN 1..5 LOOP\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (i);\nEND LOOP;\n\n-- DBSQL — iterate over a query; use range() to simulate the integer range\nFOR row AS SELECT num FROM range(1, 6) AS t(num) DO\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (row.num);\nEND FOR;\n```\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift FOR rec IN SELECT ...\nFOR rec IN SELECT id, name FROM source_table LOOP\n    UPDATE other_table SET seen = 1 WHERE id = rec.id;\nEND LOOP;\n\n-- DBSQL — same shape; `IN` becomes `AS`\nFOR rec AS SELECT id, name FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source_table DO\n    UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.other_table SET seen = 1 WHERE id = rec.id;\nEND FOR;\n```\n\nFor `FOR rec IN SELECT ... LOOP`, see _Cursors (literal translation)_ above. Prefer a set-based rewrite when possible.\n\n### Dynamic SQL\n\nRedshift `EXECUTE 'sql string' [INTO target] [USING param, ...]` → DBSQL `EXECUTE IMMEDIATE`.\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nDECLARE\n    v_sql TEXT;\n    v_result INT;\nBEGIN\n    v_sql := 'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM ' || v_table_name;\n    EXECUTE v_sql INTO v_result;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE v_sql STRING;\n    DECLARE v_result INT;\n    SET v_sql = CONCAT('SELECT COUNT(*) FROM ', v_table_name);\n    EXECUTE IMMEDIATE v_sql INTO v_result;\nEND;\n```\n\n**Parameter binding** — Redshift positional `$1`, `$2` → DBSQL `?` placeholders bound positionally via `USING`:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nEXECUTE 'SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = $1 AND status = $2' USING v_id, v_status;\n\n-- DBSQL\nEXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = ? AND status = ?' USING v_id, v_status;\n```\n\n**Dynamic table/column names** — DBSQL provides the `IDENTIFIER()` clause to safely inject an identifier from a parameter, avoiding string concatenation and SQL injection.\n\n### Dynamic SQL — untranslatable procedures\n\nIf a Redshift procedure declares a string variable, builds it across `IF`/`ELSE` branches or `LOOP` iterations, and executes it via `EXECUTE var_sql`, the entire procedure body cannot be mechanically translated — the scaffolding (variable assignments, string-building, control flow) exists only to produce the dynamic query. Do not translate pieces individually. Emit a single TODO block:\n\n```sql\n-- TODO: procedure builds and executes dynamic SQL — manual rewrite required\n```\n"}}]);
